Hello Dr. Brundage,

The Macaulay BioBlitz automatic reply directed inquiries to you.

My name is Rodrigo Castro, founder of Listening.bio, a working platform for auditable acoustic biodiversity monitoring. It accepts environmental recordings, runs BirdNET candidate detection, preserves provenance, supports confirm/reject review, and exports CSV and GeoJSON data.

Macaulay's BioBlitz model is especially relevant because Listening.bio is not intended to replace naturalists. It organizes machine-generated candidates so students and experts can inspect, correct, and learn from model errors.

I saw that the 2026 BioBlitz is planned for August 29-30 at Willowbrook Park. I do not want to add complexity to an event already in preparation. Instead, I would like to explore either a very small optional methods demonstration associated with the event, if useful and feasible, or a later collaboration that compares acoustic candidates with BioBlitz observations and creates a student review exercise.

A first study could use three to five approved sites and 50 to 100 recordings, with transparent reporting of correct detections, false candidates, and model limitations. The specific research and education questions would be shaped with your team before any recording begins.

Listening.bio can provide the software workflow, processing, review interface, maps, and research-ready exports at no software cost during the pilot. I am seeking scientific and educational feedback, help shaping the validation method, and an appropriate event or green-space context.

If this could complement Macaulay's work, would you be willing to review the one-page protocol by email and let me know which educational or scientific question would be most useful to test? Written feedback or a referral to the appropriate colleague would be very helpful. The live evidence demonstration is available at https://listening.bio.

